<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<class name="World" inherits="SceneTree" api_type="extension" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/master/doc/class.xsd">
	<brief_description>
		The world: the main loop, the level that is running and everything in it.
	</brief_description>
	<description>
		Set [code]application/run/main_loop_type[/code] to [code]World[/code] to turn the framework on. It owns what belongs to a level - the game mode, the game state and the list of controllers - while the [GameInstance] it holds owns what outlives one, which is the local players.
		It is also where the session lives: [method host_game], [method create_dedicated_server] and [method join_game] decide the [enum NetMode], and [method server_travel] moves the server and every client to a new level together. [method has_authority] is true in a game with no peer at all, which is what makes a rule written for a server also correct offline.
		The nodes it builds sit at fixed paths - [code]/root/NetDriver[/code], [code]/root/GameState[/code], [code]/root/Players[/code] and [code]/root/Pawns[/code] - because that is the address every peer refers to them by.
	</description>
